_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 15
---
order: 15
---
short_label: III.15
---
statement: Of straight lines in a circle the diameter is greatest, and of the rest the nearer to the center is always greater than the more remote.
---
proof:

Let *ABCD* be a circle, *AD* its diameter, and *E* its center.  Let *BC* be nearer to the center *AD,* and *FG* more remote.

I say that *AD* is greatest and *BC* greater than *FG.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="280" height="280" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.15 diagram" src="propIII15.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.15",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;140,35",
            "D;point;free;140,245",
            "E;point;midpoint;A,D;black;green",
            "ABCD;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;black;random",
            "AD;line;connect;A,D",
            "B;point;circleSlider;ABCD,200,60",
            "C;point;circleSlider;ABCD,170,200",
            "F;point;circleSlider;ABCD,50,80",
            "G;point;circleSlider;ABCD,40,160",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "FG;line;connect;F,G",
            "H;point;foot;E,BC",
            "K;point;foot;E,FG",
            "EH;line;connect;E,H",
            "EK;line;connect;E,K",
            "L;point;cutoff;E,K,E,H",
            "LL';line;perpendicular;L,E;0;0;0",
            "MN;line;chord;LL',ABCD",
            "M;point;first;MN",
            "N;point;last;MN",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F",
            "EG;line;connect;E,G",
            "EM;line;connect;E,M",
            "EN;line;connect;E,N"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Draw *EH* and *EK* from the center *E* perpendicular to *BC* and *FG.* [!just I.12]

Then, since *BC* is nearer to the center and *FG* more remote, *EK* is greater than *EH.* [!just III.Def.5]

Make *EL*  equal to *EH.* Draw *LM* through *L* at right angles to *EK,* and carry it through to *N.* Join *ME, EN, FE,* and *EG.* [!just I.3; I.11]

Then, since *EH* equals *EL, BC* also equals *MN.* [!just III.14]

Again, since *AE* equals *EM,* and *ED* equals *EN, AD* equals the sum of *ME* and *EN.*

But the sum of *ME* and *EN* is greater than *MN,* and *MN* equals *BC,* therefore *AD* is greater than *BC.* [!just I.20]

And, since the two sides *ME* and *EN* equal the two sides *FE* and *EG,* and the angle *MEN* greater than the angle *FEG,* therefore the base *MN* is greater than the base *FG.* [!just I.24]

But *MN* was proved equal to *BC.*

Therefore the diameter *AD* is greatest and *BC* greater than *FG.*

Therefore *of straight lines in a circle the diameter is greatest, and of the rest the nearer to the center is always greater than the more remote.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

This proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*
